#version 450
#extension GL_KHR_cooperative_matrix : require
#extension GL_KHR_memory_scope_semantics : require
#extension GL_EXT_shader_explicit_arithmetic_types_float16 : require
#extension GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_basic : require
#extension GL_EXT_control_flow_attributes : enable

// y = W * X on the matrix cores, for one Q4_0 matrix against a batch of Q8_0
// activations.
//
// **This is not bound in, and here is what it measures and why it was
// written.** llama.cpp's own Vulkan build on this card says
// `matrix cores: KHR_coopmat` in its log, and turning them off with
// GGML_VK_DISABLE_COOPMAT=1 takes its Qwen3 4B prompt from 2958 tokens a
// second to 1532. That factor of 1.93 is the largest single piece of the gap
// between golem's prompt and theirs, and this file is the attempt at it.
//
// RDNA's cores multiply a sixteen by sixteen block in one instruction. The
// trade they ask for is that both operands arrive as ordinary numbers, so this
// kernel dequantises the weights and the activation into shared memory as fp16
// and multiplies what it staged — which is what llama.cpp's own mul_mm does,
// and is why there is no correction term here where the integer kernels carry
// one: the weights are recentred by eight as they are unpacked rather than
// eight blocks later.
//
// It agrees with the CPU to a part in six thousand and it is slower than
// shaders/matmul.comp: 9.1 microseconds a column against 5.9 on the Qwen3 4B's
// ffn_down at thirty-two columns, 6.5 against nothing at all at two hundred
// and fifty-six. What that cost is made of was measured by compiling pieces of
// it away:
//
//   - **The cores are not the problem.** Repeating the multiply loop four and
//     sixteen times over the same staged tile costs 0.66 microseconds a column
//     each time, which puts the multiply at 76 TFLOP/s — the same order as
//     llama.cpp's own 70 on a 512-column product. Seven per cent of the pass.
//   - **The dequantising is not the problem either.** Compiled with the
//     unpacking replaced by a store that still depends on the block, the pass
//     goes from 9.1 to 7.8. Fourteen per cent.
//   - So about eighty per cent of it is the staging: the global reads, the
//     shared writes, and the barriers between them. That is the same eighty
//     per cent shaders/matmul.comp spends, and neither kernel has found it.
//
// What was tried against that and measured worse, so that it is not tried
// again:
//
//   - **A bigger BK.** Sixty-four, a hundred and twenty-eight and two hundred
//     and fifty-six all lose, monotonically, and the reason is that the staged
//     tile is what decides how many workgroups a compute unit holds. At BK=32
//     this kernel is under eight kilobytes and the card runs eight of them.
//   - **A bigger BM.** A hundred and twenty-eight and two hundred and
//     fifty-six lose the same way and for the same reason.
//   - **Interleaving the weights by tile** so that a workgroup's read of one
//     block is one contiguous run instead of BM sixteen-byte pieces a row
//     apart — vk/mixture.go's tileQ4_0. It is the layout this kernel reads and
//     it changed nothing measurable, which says the level two cache was
//     already absorbing the waste.
//   - **Reading the activation as fp16 straight from a buffer** instead of
//     staging it, so that forty workgroups stop dequantising the same twelve
//     million values into forty copies. Thirty-two columns got worse (11.1
//     against 9.1) and only a hundred and twenty-eight got better.
//
// **Splitting the shared dimension, and blocking the columns, were then tried,
// and they are what the sweeps above were missing.** With p.split slices of
// the shared dimension and BN columns to a workgroup rather than all of them —
// which is llama.cpp's own shape, and neither of golem's two products had it —
// this kernel reaches 2.66 microseconds a column on ffn_down at a hundred and
// twenty-eight columns, against the integer product's 4.24 at thirty-two. That
// is the first time the cores have been ahead of the dot products.
//
// **Measured again once the harness stopped lying about it, it is at parity
// and not ahead.** vk/matmul.go used to force this kernel's split to one and
// pass it no BN at all, so every number the benchmark had ever printed for it
// was of a kernel without the two things that put it ahead — and the dispatch
// did not count its column groups either, which for a kernel nothing binds is
// a bug waiting rather than a bug. With all three fixed, on the 12B at two
// hundred and fifty-six columns, which is the width the stack now carries:
// ffn_down 4.76 microseconds a column against the integer product's 4.69, and
// ffn_gate 9.01 against 8.90. The cores are level with the dot products and
// marginally behind, which is what this file's own analysis predicted — eighty
// per cent of the pass is the staging, and the staging is the same.
//
// **Its own geometry was then swept again through the fixed harness**, since
// every earlier sweep of it went through the broken one. On the 12B at two
// hundred and fifty-six columns, microseconds a column, ffn_down then
// ffn_gate: 64/32/32 gives 4.76 and 9.03, 64/64/32 gives **4.40** and 9.24,
// 128/64/32 gives 4.74 and 9.55, 64/32/64 gives 4.84 and 9.08, 128/32/32
// gives 4.52 and 9.40, 64/64/64 gives 4.87 and 9.73, 128/128/32 gives 5.85
// and 9.71. The best of them is six per cent ahead of the integer product on
// ffn_down and four per cent behind it on ffn_gate. Six per cent is not the
// 1.93 that turning coopmat off costs llama.cpp, and no geometry here finds
// it.
//
// It is still not bound in, and now for one reason rather than two. It wanted
// a pass of at least a hundred and twenty-eight columns, and the stack carries
// two hundred and fifty-six now, so that objection is gone; what remains is
// that there is nothing to win. And **ffn_gate does not move**. That shape — many rows, a
// short shared dimension, which is what every gate, up and qkv projection is —
// sits at six microseconds a column under every combination of BM, BN, BK,
// split and width measured, integer or cooperative, while ffn_down's shape
// goes to 2.66. Nothing in the arithmetic of the two is different: the same
// weights, the same activations, the same multiply count, the same staging.
// Naming that asymmetry is what the next pass at this is for.

#ifndef COLUMNS
#define COLUMNS 32
#endif

// BM rows to a workgroup, BK of the shared dimension at a time. BK is a
// multiple of thirty-two because a Q4_0 block is thirty-two values and a block
// is the unit a scale belongs to.
#ifndef BM
#define BM 64
#endif
#ifndef BK
#define BK 32
#endif
#ifndef THREADS
#define THREADS 256
#endif

// BN is how many of the pass's columns one workgroup answers. Giving a
// workgroup every column is the shape both kernels started with, and it is the
// wrong one for a matrix with many rows and a short shared dimension: there is
// nothing left to divide but the rows, and the tile wants those. Cutting the
// columns as well multiplies the workgroups without the reduction a split of
// the shared dimension needs — each one writes outputs nobody else writes. It
// costs reading the weights COLUMNS/BN times.
#ifndef BN
#define BN COLUMNS
#endif

// WAVE is the width vk/device.go asks the driver for, and the reason it has to
// be asked for rather than taken: WAVES below is how many row blocks each wave
// owns, and an accumulator array indexed by anything the compiler cannot fold
// is not in registers at all. It is in scratch memory, and a cooperative
// matrix in scratch memory is the difference between a kernel that runs at the
// cores' speed and one that runs at a fifth of it.
#ifndef WAVE
#define WAVE 64
#endif
#define WAVES (THREADS / WAVE)

// The cooperative matrix is sixteen by sixteen by sixteen, which is the only
// shape this driver offers. RDNA has others in hardware; the extension does
// not expose them.
#define TILE 16

// The staged operands, padded so that a wave's lanes do not land on one bank.
// Shared memory is thirty-two banks of four bytes and these are two-byte
// values, so the padding is eight of them.
#define SA_STRIDE (BK + 8)
#define SB_STRIDE (BK + 8)

layout(local_size_x = THREADS) in;

layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly  buffer W  { uint  w[];   };
layout(std430, binding = 1) readonly  buffer AQ { uint  aq[];  };  // the activation, Q8_0
layout(std430, binding = 2) readonly  buffer AS { float as_[]; };  // its scales, then its corrections
layout(std430, binding = 3) writeonly buffer Y  { float y[];   };


layout(push_constant) uniform Push {
    uint dim;   // outputs
    uint ffn;   // inputs
    uint used;  // one, and only here so that every kernel takes the same block
    uint act;   // the activation, which is spent before this kernel runs
    uint col;   // the gate kernel's, and not read here
    uint split; // slices of the shared dimension, one for the whole of it
} p;

shared float16_t sa[BM * SA_STRIDE];
shared float16_t sb[BN * SB_STRIDE];

// The answer goes straight out. A cooperative matrix is stored to a stride the
// whole sixteen-row tile has to exist for, which is why vk/matmul.go refuses a
// matrix whose row count is not a multiple of sixteen: with that, a row block
// is either wholly inside the matrix or wholly outside it, and the one below
// skips the outside ones.

void main() {
    uint t    = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    uint nb   = p.ffn >> 5;          // blocks of the shared dimension
    // The slice of the shared dimension this workgroup answers. A tile this
    // wide leaves ffn_down twenty workgroups, which is a third of the compute
    // units; the split is what puts them back.
    uint wgs  = (p.dim + uint(BM) - 1u) / uint(BM);
    const uint CGROUPS = uint(COLUMNS) / uint(BN);
    uint ir   = gl_WorkGroupID.x % wgs;
    uint ic   = (gl_WorkGroupID.x / wgs) % CGROUPS;
    uint ik   = gl_WorkGroupID.x / (wgs * CGROUPS);
    uint row0 = ir * uint(BM);
    uint col0 = ic * uint(BN);
    uint aw   = p.ffn >> 2;          // one column of the activation, in uints
    uint rowBytes = nb * 18u;
    const uint KB = uint(BK) >> 5;   // Q4_0 blocks in one step
    uint per  = ((nb + p.split - 1u) / p.split + KB - 1u) & ~(KB - 1u);
    uint kb0  = ik * per;
    uint kbn  = min(nb, kb0 + per);

    // The tile this wave owns: RSUB row blocks by every column block, which
    // are static counts, so the accumulators stay in registers.
    const uint RB = uint(BM) / uint(TILE);
    const uint CB = uint(BN) / uint(TILE);
    const uint RSUB = RB / uint(WAVES);
    uint rbase = gl_SubgroupID * RSUB;

    coopmat<float, gl_ScopeSubgroup, TILE, TILE, gl_MatrixUseAccumulator> acc[RSUB][CB];
    [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < RSUB; r++) {
        [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < CB; c++) {
            acc[r][c] = coopmat<float, gl_ScopeSubgroup, TILE, TILE, gl_MatrixUseAccumulator>(0.0);
        }
    }

    for (uint kb = kb0; kb < kbn; kb += KB) {
        barrier();

        // The weights, dequantised.
        //
        // The layout is vk/mixture.go's tileQ4_0: this workgroup's BM rows are
        // interleaved, so one block of the shared dimension is BM fp16 scales
        // and then BM sets of sixteen nibble bytes, all of it one run. A wave
        // reading it reads consecutive addresses, which is the difference
        // between using a cache line and using an eighth of one.
        //
        // A thread takes one uint of nibbles, which is eight weights: four in
        // the low nibbles at k, four in the high nibbles sixteen further on.
        // That is the Q4_0 layout, not a choice.
        for (uint idx = t; idx < BM * KB * 4u; idx += uint(THREADS)) {
            uint u    = idx & 3u;
            uint rest = idx >> 2;
            uint r    = rest & (uint(BM) - 1u);
            uint k    = rest / uint(BM);
            uint b    = kb + k;
            uint word = 0u;
            float ws = 0.0;
            if (row0 + r < p.dim && b < nb) {
                uint base = (ir * nb + b) * uint(BM) * 18u;
                word = w[((base + uint(BM) * 2u + r * 16u) >> 2) + u];
                ws   = unpackHalf2x16(w[(base + 2u * r) >> 2])[r & 1u];
            }
            uint at = r * uint(SA_STRIDE) + k * 32u + u * 4u;
            [[unroll]] for (uint m = 0u; m < 4u; m++) {
                uint byte = (word >> (8u * m)) & 0xFFu;
                sa[at + m]       = float16_t((float(byte & 0x0Fu) - 8.0) * ws);
                sa[at + 16u + m] = float16_t((float(byte >> 4)    - 8.0) * ws);
            }
        }

        // The activation, dequantised. A thread takes one uint, four
        // magnitudes, signed. This one does pass through shared memory: read
        // straight from a buffer instead, as fp16 written once by the kernel
        // upstream, a thirty-two column pass measured worse and only a
        // hundred-and-twenty-eight column pass measured better.
        for (uint idx = t; idx < BN * KB * 8u; idx += uint(THREADS)) {
            uint u    = idx & 7u;
            uint rest = idx >> 3;
            uint k    = rest % KB;
            uint c    = rest / KB;
            uint b    = kb + k;
            uint word = 0u;
            float as = 0.0;
            if (b < nb) {
                word = aq[(col0 + c) * aw + b * 8u + u];
                as   = as_[(col0 + c) * 2u * nb + b];
            }
            uint base = c * uint(SB_STRIDE) + k * 32u + u * 4u;
            [[unroll]] for (uint m = 0u; m < 4u; m++) {
                int q = int((word >> (8u * m)) & 0xFFu);
                if (q > 127) q -= 256;
                sb[base + m] = float16_t(float(q) * as);
            }
        }

        barrier();

        // The cores.
        //
        // The order of these three loops is the kernel. A cooperative load and
        // a cooperative multiply cost about the same, so a loop that loads
        // both operands for every multiply spends half its time loading and
        // reaches a fraction of what the cores can do. The K step is outermost
        // and the column fragments are loaded once into registers there; then
        // each row block loads its own fragment once and meets all of them.
        // That is CB + RB loads for RB * CB multiplies instead of two for one.
        [[unroll]] for (uint kk = 0u; kk < uint(BK) / uint(TILE); kk++) {
            coopmat<float16_t, gl_ScopeSubgroup, TILE, TILE, gl_MatrixUseB> B[CB];
            [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < CB; c++) {
                // The activation is one column after another, so a K by N
                // block of it is column major.
                coopMatLoad(B[c], sb, c * uint(TILE) * uint(SB_STRIDE) + kk * uint(TILE),
                            uint(SB_STRIDE), gl_CooperativeMatrixLayoutColumnMajor);
            }
            [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < RSUB; r++) {
                coopmat<float16_t, gl_ScopeSubgroup, TILE, TILE, gl_MatrixUseA> A;
                coopMatLoad(A, sa, (rbase + r) * uint(TILE) * uint(SA_STRIDE) + kk * uint(TILE),
                            uint(SA_STRIDE), gl_CooperativeMatrixLayoutRowMajor);
                [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < CB; c++) {
                    acc[r][c] = coopMatMulAdd(A, B[c], acc[r][c]);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < RSUB; r++) {
        uint row = row0 + (rbase + r) * uint(TILE);
        if (row >= p.dim) continue;
        [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < CB; c++) {
            coopMatStore(acc[r][c], y, (ik * uint(COLUMNS) + col0 + c * uint(TILE)) * p.dim + row,
                         p.dim, gl_CooperativeMatrixLayoutColumnMajor);
        }
    }
}
